1
00:00:04,850 --> 00:00:07,189
REMI: Pip! Pip!

2
00:00:08,050 --> 00:00:15,830
At fourteen minutes past six on a Monday evening, Remi Hale would have given anything to have that morning back.

3
00:00:18,050 --> 00:00:20,443
She was about to get it.

4
00:00:39,250 --> 00:00:45,050
Twelve hours earlier. Remi had one job that Monday. Be on time.

5
00:00:45,050 --> 00:00:50,060
RADIO: Seven o’clock, Monday morning. Rain by six.

6
00:00:52,250 --> 00:00:56,731
REMI: Seven forty? No. No, no, no!

7
00:00:58,650 --> 00:01:04,850
PIP: Remi! Will you watch me ride at the park? Six o’clock!

8
00:01:04,850 --> 00:01:08,705
REMI: Can’t, Pip. Big day. Sorry.

9
00:01:17,050 --> 00:01:21,496
She missed the tram. Then she missed the next one.

10
00:01:27,650 --> 00:01:30,924
INTERVIEWER: You’re forty minutes late, Ms Hale.

11
00:01:32,050 --> 00:01:37,222
INTERVIEWER: Tell me. If you could build anything at all, what would you build?

12
00:01:37,950 --> 00:01:42,177
REMI: I… um. I’m sorry. I don’t know.

13
00:01:42,250 --> 00:01:44,393
INTERVIEWER: We’ll be in touch.

14
00:01:46,050 --> 00:01:48,669
They would not be in touch.

15
00:01:52,250 --> 00:01:58,847
On the way home it rained, and Remi ducked into a clock shop she had never noticed before.

16
00:02:00,250 --> 00:02:05,869
WATCHMAKER: Ah. A bad day. I have just the thing.

17
00:02:06,250 --> 00:02:16,240
WATCHMAKER: Turn this knob before you sleep, and you’ll wake up to the same morning. Seven turns. After that, it’s only a clock.

18
00:02:16,450 --> 00:02:18,291
REMI: How much?

19
00:02:19,250 --> 00:02:22,328
WATCHMAKER: Whatever you think a day is worth.

20
00:02:28,250 --> 00:02:38,450
One of the first alarm clocks in America was built in 1787. It could only ring at four in the morning, the hour its maker got up for work.

21
00:02:38,450 --> 00:02:43,273
Remi’s could do something stranger. She turned the knob.

22
00:02:47,450 --> 00:02:52,450
RADIO: Seven o’clock, Monday morning. Rain by six.

23
00:02:52,450 --> 00:02:55,133
REMI: Monday. Again.

24
00:02:56,050 --> 00:03:03,450
About two in three people say they have felt déjà vu at least once. Remi was feeling it on purpose.

25
00:03:03,450 --> 00:03:12,141
Your body keeps its own clock: about twenty thousand nerve cells, just above the place where your optic nerves cross.

26
00:03:13,250 --> 00:03:21,148
Left alone, it runs about eleven minutes longer than a day. Every morning, the light puts it right again.

27
00:03:24,650 --> 00:03:30,650
PIP: Remi! Will you watch me ride at the park? Six o’clock!

28
00:03:30,650 --> 00:03:33,835
REMI: Big day, Pip. Next time.

29
00:03:36,450 --> 00:03:39,225
So she practised Monday.

30
00:03:39,250 --> 00:03:43,014
REMI: A bridge. No. A tower?

31
00:03:44,650 --> 00:03:52,850
Once you know how something turns out, it feels as if you knew it all along. Psychologists call it hindsight bias.

32
00:03:52,850 --> 00:03:57,169
Remi didn’t just feel it. She really did know.

33
00:04:10,150 --> 00:04:14,173
REMI: A school where every classroom gets the morning sun.

34
00:04:15,050 --> 00:04:24,034
INTERVIEWER: Ms Hale, that’s the best answer I’ve heard all year. Come back at five. The director wants to meet you.

35
00:04:24,450 --> 00:04:28,215
REMI: Yes! I mean, yes. Thank you.

36
00:04:32,450 --> 00:04:39,566
The final round ran late. Remi didn’t look at the time. It was the perfect Monday.

37
00:04:40,250 --> 00:04:46,950
At home, Pip had made her a good luck card. And Pip knew the way to the tram stop.

38
00:05:00,850 --> 00:05:04,566
REMI: This is my fault. This is all my fault.

39
00:05:05,250 --> 00:05:12,159
Pip would be all right, the doctors said. In time. But Remi still had turns left.

40
00:05:13,850 --> 00:05:18,058
That night, she turned the knob with shaking hands.

41
00:05:24,250 --> 00:05:28,786
REMI: Pip. Promise me you’ll stay home tonight. Promise.

42
00:05:29,450 --> 00:05:33,530
PIP: Okay, okay! I promise.

43
00:05:35,250 --> 00:05:38,228
REMI: Pick up. Pip, pick up.

44
00:05:39,050 --> 00:05:46,439
Pip kept her promise. Until she saw the rain, and remembered her sister had no umbrella.

45
00:05:57,650 --> 00:06:03,404
REMI: It keeps happening! Whatever I change, it happens at fourteen past six!

46
00:06:03,650 --> 00:06:08,618
WATCHMAKER: Because you keep the day you wanted, and only try to change the ending.

47
00:06:10,250 --> 00:06:18,702
WATCHMAKER: A day isn’t a recording, miss. It’s a promise. You only get to choose who it’s for.

48
00:06:19,250 --> 00:06:29,050
Every night, your brain replays the day by itself. In rats, scientists watched brain cells repeat the routes of the day while the animals slept.

49
00:06:29,050 --> 00:06:32,850
Replaying is the easy part. Choosing is hard.

50
00:06:32,850 --> 00:06:36,275
REMI: This is the last Monday I can fix.

51
00:06:36,650 --> 00:06:40,573
WATCHMAKER: Then don’t fix it. Live it.

52
00:06:42,650 --> 00:06:48,850
PIP: Remi! Will you watch me ride at the park? Six o’clock!

53
00:06:48,850 --> 00:06:53,050
REMI: Six o’clock. I’ll be there. I promise.

54
00:06:53,050 --> 00:06:54,658
PIP: Really?

55
00:06:56,650 --> 00:07:01,510
INTERVIEWER: Come back at five. The director wants to meet you.

56
00:07:01,650 --> 00:07:06,241
REMI: I’m sorry. I can’t. I made a promise.

57
00:07:06,250 --> 00:07:09,771
INTERVIEWER: Then I’m afraid there won’t be a second chance.

58
00:07:11,350 --> 00:07:13,083
REMI: I know.

59
00:07:30,650 --> 00:07:32,871
REMI: Pip, wait!

60
00:07:35,050 --> 00:07:38,250
PIP: Whoa. That was close.

61
00:07:38,250 --> 00:07:40,082
PIP: You came!

62
00:07:40,450 --> 00:07:42,720
REMI: I’ll always come.

63
00:07:50,250 --> 00:07:55,596
That night, Remi had one turn left. She didn’t take it.

64
00:08:12,050 --> 00:08:17,386
RADIO: Seven o’clock, Tuesday morning. Sunny all day.

65
00:08:17,650 --> 00:08:19,427
REMI: Tuesday.

66
00:08:22,650 --> 00:08:31,457
VOICEMAIL: Ms Hale, it’s Halden Architects. The director heard why you left. She’d like to meet you on Thursday.

67
00:08:41,650 --> 00:08:46,234
Remi never turned the knob again. She didn’t need to.

68
00:08:46,650 --> 00:08:52,441
You can’t rewind a day. But every morning, you get to choose who it’s for.
